w.append("**Reading, including power.** This test has far more discordant folds than Monty-ARC's (265 vs 54 on ConceptARC, 300 vs 126 on 1D-ARC). A shift of Qwen's size, +5.2, would separate on ConceptARC (half-width 3.3) and 1D-ARC (0.9), is about the width of the interval on Mini-ARC (5.3), and would not separate on MinimalTasks (8.4). **On ConceptARC the two statistics disagree:** the planned interval just includes 0, while the cluster sign test gives 27:54, p = 0.004. If anything real is there, test outputs are *easier* than demonstrations for this program, the opposite sign to Qwen on ARC-AGI-2 and to Monty-ARC's lean on the same set (+0.9), so the direction depends on the learner. I report it as a possible failure of (E), not an established one, with no correction for multiplicity. Test and demonstration inputs are about the same size (115 vs 116 cells, post hoc), so size does not explain it. Mini-ARC and 1D-ARC show no difference in rates; 1D-ARC by task leans the same way only in the sign test (95:126). Judged: tests easier than demonstrations would mean fewer confident errors on the test role, not more, so this direction would not threaten the bound. ARC-AGI-2 has almost nothing to compare: 8 discordant folds. So this learner **neither confirms nor contradicts** Qwen's finding there.\n")
